17592186125000 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125000 #edb/ref 1071 "Which of the following statements are true about the function $f(x,y,z)=\\frac{{z}^{2}}{{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}?$\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** The partial derivative with respect to $x$ is defined at $(1,-1,0)$\n- **II.** ${f}_{x}(0,1,1)$ is defined\n- **III.** ${f}_{z}(1,1,0)$ is defined" #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186125000 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "ff5209de-df39-5705-8c96-c53acb60628b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125000 #edb/ref 1074 "First, we compute the partial derivatives of $f(x,y,z):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & =-\\frac{{z}^{2}}{({x}^{2}-{y}^{2})^{2}}\\cdot (2x) \\\\  & =-\\frac{2x{z}^{2}}{({x}^{2}-{y}^{2})^{2}} \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂y} & =-\\frac{{z}^{2}}{({x}^{2}-{y}^{2})^{2}}\\cdot (-2y) \\\\  & =\\frac{2y{z}^{2}}{({x}^{2}-{y}^{2})^{2}} \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂z} & =\\frac{2z}{{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's examine the statements one-by-one.\n\n- Statement I is false. If we substitute $(1,-1,0)$ into $f$, we get\n\n$$\nf(1,-1,0)=\\frac{(0)^{2}}{(1)^{2}+(-1)^{2}}=\\frac{0}{0},\n$$\n\nwhich is undefined since we have division by zero. Since the function is not defined at the point, the derivative is also undefined.\n\n- Statement II is true. Indeed, the function $f$ is defined at $(0,1,1)$, where we have \n\n$$\nf(0,1,1)=\\frac{(1)^{2}}{(0)^{2}-(1)^{2}}=-1,\n$$\n\nand ${f}_{x}$ is also defined at $(0,1,1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{x}(0,1,1) & =-\\frac{2(0)(1)}{(0)^{2}-(1)^{2}}=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false. If we substitute $(1,1,0)$ into $f$, we get\n\n$$\nf(1,1,0)=\\frac{(0)^{2}}{(1)^{2}-(1)^{2}}=\\frac{0}{0},\n$$\n\nwhich is undefined since we have division by zero. Since the function is not defined at the point, the derivative is also undefined.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is 'II only'." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125311 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186125312 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125311 #edb/ref 1061 11425 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125311 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "e4bac5fb-b77d-59b1-8380-2ff0cf754d5c" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125311 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186125741 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125312 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186125463 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125312 #edb/ref 1061 11426 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125312 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "d81b9ea9-a347-5ebb-91d1-4bc5c4b566e0" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125312 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186124465 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125313 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186125314 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125313 #edb/ref 1061 11429 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125313 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "f4cc9d43-0b3e-5cd2-8e04-c1f0f43e9e2a" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125313 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186124466 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125314 #edb/ref 1061 11432 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125314 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "f23e88d8-c204-5795-b172-b05000a202c3" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125314 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186125742 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125463 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186125313 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125463 #edb/ref 1061 22675 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125463 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "c86dfe84-cedf-55cf-bf44-efb12a49ac66" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125463 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186124557 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125741 #edb/ref 1064 "The partial derivative of a function exists where the original function is defined *and* the partial derivative is defined.\n\nFor example, let's consider the function $f(x,y) = \\ln(xy).$ We wish to determine the set of values $(x,y)$ on which the partial derivative $\\dfrac{\\partial f}{\\partial x}$ exists.\n\nFirst, we need to find the domain of $f$ since we cannot differentiate $f$ at any point that lies outside of its domain. The natural logarithm is defined only for positive inputs, so the domain of $f$ is \n\n$$\nD_f = \\{ (x,y)\\, : \\, xy > 0 \\}.\n$$\n\nNext, we compute the partial derivative:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\partial f}{\\partial x} = \\dfrac{1}{x}\n$$\n\nThe partial derivative $\\dfrac{\\partial f}{\\partial x}$ (also denoted $f_x$) is not defined at $x=0.$ So, the domain of $f_x$ is\n\n$$\nD_{f_x} = \\{ (x,y)\\, : \\, x \\ne 0 \\}.\n$$\n\nTo compute the set where  $\\dfrac{\\partial f}{\\partial x}$ exists, we take the intersection of the domain of the original function $(D_f)$ with the domain of the partial derivative $(D_{f_x})$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{D}_{f}\\cap {D}_{{f}_{x}} & =\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,xy>0\\}\\cap \\{(x,y)\\,:\\,x\\ne 0\\} \\\\  & =\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,xy>0\\}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that $\\dfrac{\\partial f}{\\partial x}$ exists on the set $\\{ (x,y)\\, : \\, xy \\gt 0 \\}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125741 #edb/ref 1065 2532 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125741 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "5862db65-6f2d-5256-968d-cb971dd6b2f1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125741 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125742 #edb/ref 1064 "From single-variable calculus, if a function $y=f(x)$ is differentiable at a point, then it is continuous at that point.\n\nBut for a multivariable function, even if all partial derivatives are defined at a certain point, it **does not mean** that the function is continuous at that point!\n\nThe reason why partial differentiability does not imply continuity in the case of a multivariable function is that\n\n- the existence of the partial derivatives $f_x$ and $f_y$ depend only on the behavior of $f$ parallel to the $x$- and $y$-axes, whereas\n\n- the continuity of $f$ depends on its behavior as we approach $(x_0,y_0)$ from any direction.\n\nFor example, let's consider the following multivariable function:\n\n![](images/cb/cb5aad599789d1e317ff3ddc07eaa418e80469307f67e98e2108660fc7d15701.svg)\n\nThe function $f$ is defined at the point $(0,0)\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\nf(0,0)=0\n$$\n\nLikewise, the partial derivatives $f_x$ and $f_y$ are both defined at the point $(0,0).$ We can compute them using the limit definition of the partial derivative, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{x}(0,0) & ={lim}_{h\\to 0}\\frac{1}{h}[f(0+h,0)-f(0,0)] \\\\  & ={lim}_{h\\to 0}\\frac{1}{h}[\\frac{(h)(0)}{{h}^{2}+{0}^{2}}-0] \\\\  & ={lim}_{h\\to 0}\\frac{1}{h}[0-0] \\\\  & =0 \\\\ {f}_{y}(0,0) & ={lim}_{h\\to 0}\\frac{1}{h}[f(0,0+h)-f(0,0)] \\\\  & ={lim}_{h\\to 0}\\frac{1}{h}[\\frac{(0)(h)}{{0}^{2}+{h}^{2}}-0] \\\\  & ={lim}_{h\\to 0}\\frac{1}{h}[0-0] \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo the partial derivatives both exist at $(0,0).$ We will now show that the function is not continuous at $(0,0).$\n\nFor the function $f$ to be continuous at $(0,0),$ the limit of the function must be equal to the function value, regardless of the path taken. So, we must have\n\n$$\n\\lim\\limits_{(x,y) \\to (0,0)}  f(x,y) = f(0,0) =0.\n$$\n\nHowever, this is not the case here. When we evaluate the limit along the path $y=x,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{(x,y)\\to (0,0)\\,\\,y=x}f(x,y) & ={lim}_{y\\to 0}\\frac{x\\cdot x}{{x}^{2}+{x}^{2}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{y\\to 0}\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2{x}^{2}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{y\\to 0}\\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & \\ne 0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our function $f(x,y)$ is *not* continuous at the point $(0,0),$ even though both of its partial derivatives exist there." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125742 #edb/ref 1065 2533 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125742 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "ca47070b-a1d3-53ad-8f57-2dbd4e770dce" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125742 #edb/ref 1067 "Partial Differentiability Does Not Imply Continuity" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186268559 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186493443 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186268559 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186493462 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186268559 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186493517 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186268559 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186493770 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186268559 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186493778 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186268559 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186268559 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186268559 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186493462 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186268559 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "544bcd11-f607-53b0-99d1-1273ed387e89" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186338315 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186559016 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186338315 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186559174 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186338315 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186559289 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186338315 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186559318 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186338315 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186559581 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186338315 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186338315 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186338315 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186559174 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186338315 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "167ce942-9903-5739-b46e-634adbd1db2c" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186338495 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186559249 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186338495 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186559277 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186338495 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186559374 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186338495 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186559381 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186338495 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186559473 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186338495 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186338495 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186338495 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186559277 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186338495 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "7bcd04be-945c-5dcb-a1c2-bb0c92803f98" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186338583 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186559186 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186338583 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186559239 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186338583 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186559501 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186338583 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186559513 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186338583 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186559521 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186338583 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186338583 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186338583 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186559239 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186338583 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "a3e9914c-1c1b-5fbc-89f1-1c2bcbc35ca0" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186338720 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186559185 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186338720 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186559246 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186338720 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186559284 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186338720 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186559343 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186338720 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186559636 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186338720 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186338720 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186338720 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186559246 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186338720 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "fafcaa7d-c351-5f15-a3a5-2501dd663ad5" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186339043 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186338315 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186339043 #edb/ref 1069 "q-72005" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186339043 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186339043 #edb/ref 1071 "Given that $g(x,y)=x\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})$ and\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂g}{∂x}=\\frac{2{x}^{2}}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}+\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}),\\,\\frac{∂g}{∂y}=\\frac{2xy}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}},\n$$\n\nfind the set of values $(x,y)$ where all partial derivatives of $g$ exist." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186339043 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "329fb25a-c39e-587a-8c51-720f867dc9e6" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186339043 #edb/ref 1074 "First, notice that $g(x,y)=x\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})$ is defined only when ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}>0.$ So, the domain of $g$ is \n\n$$\n{D}_{g}=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}>0\\}.\n$$\n\nAs for the partial derivatives, we have the following.\n\n- The function ${g}_{x}(x,y)=\\frac{2{x}^{2}}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}+\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})$ is defined only when ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}>0.$ So, the domain of ${g}_{x}$ is \n\n$$\n{D}_{{g}_{x}}=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}>0\\}.\n$$\n\n- The function ${g}_{y}(x,y)=\\frac{2xy}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}$ is defined only when ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}\\ne 0.$ So, the domain of ${g}_{y}$ is \n\n$$\n{D}_{{g}_{y}}=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}\\ne 0\\}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we conclude that the partials of $g$ exist on the set\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{D}_{g}\\cap {D}_{{g}_{x}}\\cap {D}_{{g}_{y}} & =\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}>0\\}\\cap \\{(x,y)\\,:\\,{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}>0\\}\\cap \\{(x,y)\\,:\\,{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}\\ne 0\\} \\\\  & =\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}>0\\}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186339044 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186338495 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186339044 #edb/ref 1069 "q-72008" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186339044 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186339044 #edb/ref 1071 "Given that $g(x,y)=\\sqrt{x}\\,{e}^{x/y}$ and\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂g}{∂x}=\\frac{(2x+y){e}^{x/y}}{2y\\sqrt{x}},\\,\\frac{∂g}{∂y}=-\\frac{\\sqrt{{x}^{3}}\\,{e}^{x/y}}{{y}^{2}},\n$$\n\nfind the set of values $(x,y)$ where all partial derivatives of $g$ exist." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186339044 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "af4cc0a9-980f-5d30-aeb4-1f5c6677cbac" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186339044 #edb/ref 1074 "First, notice that $g(x,y)=\\sqrt{x}\\,{e}^{x/y}$ is defined only when $x\\ge 0$ and $y\\ne 0.$ So, the domain of $g$ is \n\n$$\n{D}_{g}=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,x\\ge 0,y\\ne 0\\}.\n$$\n\nAs for the partial derivatives, we have the following.\n\n- The function ${g}_{x}(x,y)=\\frac{(2x+y){e}^{x/y}}{2y\\sqrt{x}}$ is defined only when $x>0$ and $y\\ne 0.$ So, the domain of ${g}_{x}$ is \n\n$$\n{D}_{{g}_{x}}=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,x>0,y\\ne 0\\}.\n$$\n\n- The function ${g}_{y}(x,y)=-\\frac{\\sqrt{{x}^{3}}{e}^{x/y}}{{y}^{2}}$ is defined only when $x\\ge 0$ and $y\\ne 0.$ So, the domain of ${g}_{x}$ is \n\n$$\n{D}_{{g}_{y}}=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,x\\ge 0,y\\ne 0\\}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we conclude that the partials of $g$ exist on the set\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{D}_{g}\\cap {D}_{{g}_{x}}\\cap {D}_{{g}_{y}} & =\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,x\\ge 0,y\\ne 0\\}\\cap \\{(x,y)\\,:\\,x>0,y\\ne 0\\}\\cap \\{(x,y)\\,:\\,x\\ge 0,y\\ne 0\\} \\\\  & =\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,x>0,y\\ne 0\\}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186339045 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186452628 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186339045 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186452629 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186339045 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186452630 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186339045 #edb/ref 1069 "q-72022" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186339045 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186339045 #edb/ref 1071 "Which of the following statements are true about the function $f(x,y,z)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(xy(z-1))?$\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** The partial derivative with respect to $z$ is defined at $(3,2,1)$\n\n- **II.** ${f}_{y}(1,1,2)$ is defined\n\n- **III.** ${f}_{x}(1,2,3)$ is defined" #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186339045 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "ada5bc24-8a74-58e6-b57b-4b21dfa8df4f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186339045 #edb/ref 1074 "First, we compute the partial derivatives of $f(x,y,z):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & =\\frac{1}{xy(z-1)}\\cdot y(z-1) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{x} \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂y} & =\\frac{1}{xy(z-1)}\\cdot x(z-1) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{y} \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂z} & =\\frac{1}{xy(z-1)}\\cdot xy \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{z-1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's examine the statements one-by-one.  \n\n- Statement I is false. If we substitute $(3,2,1)$ into $f$, we get\n\n$$\nf(3,2,1)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3\\cdot 2\\cdot (1-1))=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(0),\n$$\n\nwhich is undefined.\n\n- Statement II is true. If we substitute $(1,1,2)$ into $f$, we get\n\n$$\nf(1,1,2)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1\\cdot 1\\cdot (2-1))=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1)=0,\n$$\n\nand ${f}_{y}$ is also defined at $(1,1,2):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{y}(1,1,2) & =\\frac{1}{1}=1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement III is true. If we substitute $(1,2,3)$ into $f$, we get\n\n$$\nf(1,2,3)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1\\cdot 2\\cdot (3-1))=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(4),\n$$\n\nand ${f}_{x}$ is also defined at $(1,2,3):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{x}(1,2,3) & =\\frac{1}{1}=1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is 'II and III  only'." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186339046 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186338583 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186339046 #edb/ref 1069 "q-72025" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186339046 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186339046 #edb/ref 1071 "Given that $f(x,y,z)=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(x+y+z)$ and\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂f}{∂x}=\\frac{∂f}{∂y}=\\frac{∂f}{∂z}=\\frac{1}{1+(x+y+z)^{2}},\n$$\n\nfind the set of values $(x,y,z)$ on which all partial derivatives of $f$ exist." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186339046 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "a87fd165-97e3-5aaa-8695-7d41aa3480e0" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186339046 #edb/ref 1074 "First, notice that $f(x,y,z)=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(x+y+z)$ is defined for any $x$, $y$, and $z.$ So, the domain of $f$ is \n\n$$\n{D}_{f}=\\{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,x,y,z\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )\\}.\n$$\n\nAs for the partial derivatives, we have the following.\n\n- The function ${f}_{x}=\\frac{1}{1+(x+y+z)^{2}}$ is defined for any $x$, $y$, and $z.$ So, the domain of ${f}_{x}$ is \n\n$$\n{D}_{{f}_{x}}=\\{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,x,y,z\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )\\}.\n$$\n\n- Similarly, the domains of ${f}_{y}$ and ${f}_{z}$ are\n\n$$\n{D}_{{f}_{y}}={D}_{{f}_{z}}=\\{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,x,y,z\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )\\}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the partial derivatives of $f$ exist on the set\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{D}_{f}\\cap {D}_{{f}_{x}}\\cap {D}_{{f}_{y}}\\cap {D}_{{f}_{z}} & ={D}_{f}\\cap {D}_{{f}_{x}} \\\\  & =\\{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,x,y,z\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )\\}\\cap \\{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,x,y,z\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )\\} \\\\  & =\\{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,x,y,z\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )\\}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186339047 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186338720 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186339047 #edb/ref 1069 "q-72027" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186339047 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186339047 #edb/ref 1071 "Given that $f(x,y,z)=\\frac{z}{\\sqrt{1+y-{x}^{2}}}$ and\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂f}{∂x}=\\frac{xz}{\\sqrt{(1+y-{x}^{2})^{3}}},\\,\\frac{∂f}{∂y}=-\\frac{z}{2\\sqrt{(1+y-{x}^{2})^{3}}},\\,\\frac{∂f}{∂z}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1+y-{x}^{2}}},\n$$\n\nfind the set of values $(x,y,z)$ on which all partial derivatives of $f$ exist." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186339047 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "b7c74b46-8f2a-5a21-a1ce-e71252b0fbf4" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186339047 #edb/ref 1074 "First, notice that $f(x,y,z)=\\frac{z}{\\sqrt{1+y-{x}^{2}}}$ is defined only when $1+y-{x}^{2}>0.$ So, the domain of $f$ is \n\n$$\n{D}_{f}=\\{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,{x}^{2}<y+1\\}.\n$$\n\nAs for the partial derivatives, we have the following.\n\n- The function ${f}_{x}=\\frac{xz}{\\sqrt{(1+y-{x}^{2})^{3}}}$ is defined only when $1+y-{x}^{2}>0.$ So, the domain of ${f}_{x}$ is \n\n$$\n{D}_{{f}_{x}}=\\{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,{x}^{2}<y+1\\}.\n$$\n\n- Similarly, the domain of ${f}_{y}$ and ${f}_{z}$ are the same\n\n$$\n{D}_{{f}_{y}}={D}_{{f}_{z}}=\\{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,{x}^{2}<y+1\\}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we conclude that the partials of $f$ exist on the set\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{D}_{f}\\cap {D}_{{f}_{x}}\\cap {D}_{{f}_{y}}\\cap {D}_{{f}_{z}} & ={D}_{f}\\cap {D}_{{f}_{x}} \\\\  & =\\{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,{x}^{2}<y+1\\}\\cap \\{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,{x}^{2}<y+1\\} \\\\  & =\\{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,{x}^{2}<y+1\\}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186382186 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186382187 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186382186 #edb/ref 1069 "q-71999" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186382186 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186382186 #edb/ref 1071 "Given that $f(x,y)=\\frac{\\sqrt{x+y}}{y},$ find the set of values $(x,y)$ on which $\\frac{∂f}{∂y}$ exists." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186382186 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "085f5bf8-fd9d-5a03-95fc-bd2093188160" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186382186 #edb/ref 1074 "First, notice that $f(x,y)$ is defined only when \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x+y & \\ge 0\\,\\text{and}\\,y\\ne 0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the domain of $f$ is \n\n$$\n{D}_{f}=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,y\\ne 0\\,\\text{and}\\,x+y\\ge 0\\}.\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the partial derivative of $f(x,y)$ with respect to $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{y}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\frac{\\sqrt{x+y}}{y}) \\\\  & =\\frac{y\\cdot \\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{x+y}}-\\sqrt{x+y}}{{y}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{y-2x-2y}{2{y}^{2}\\sqrt{x+y}} \\\\  & =\\frac{-2x-y}{2{y}^{2}\\sqrt{x+y}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe function ${f}_{y}(x,y)$ is defined only for \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y\\ne 0\\,\\text{and}\\,x+y>0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n So, the domain of ${f}_{y}$ is\n\n$$\n{D}_{{f}_{y}}=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,y\\ne 0\\,\\text{and}\\,x+y>0\\}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we conclude that the partial derivative of $f$ with respect to $y$ exists on the set\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{D}_{f}\\cap {D}_{{f}_{y}} & =\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,y\\ne 0\\,\\text{and}\\,x+y\\ge 0\\}\\cap \\{(x,y)\\,:\\,y\\ne 0\\,\\text{and}\\,x+y>0\\} \\\\  & =\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,y\\ne 0\\,\\text{and}\\,x+y>0\\}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186382187 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186382188 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186382187 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186559071 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186382187 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186559162 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186382187 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186559324 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186382187 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186559456 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186382187 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186382187 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186382187 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186559456 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186382187 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "7e5cd076-93d9-5103-82f2-702cb556fcbd" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186382188 #edb/ref 1080 "${f}_{y}$ exists everywhere on the $xy$-plane" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186382188 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186382188 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "c2bb0473-f9ad-5af8-a6db-3dea346a4532" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186382200 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186382201 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186382200 #edb/ref 1069 "q-72002" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186382200 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186382200 #edb/ref 1071 "Given that $f(x,y)=\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(xy),$ find the set of values $(x,y)$ on which $\\frac{∂f}{∂y}$ exists." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186382200 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "4dd0e620-fdf6-56fb-b1ff-11292db39e41" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186382200 #edb/ref 1074 "First, notice that $f(x,y)$ is defined when $-1\\le xy\\le 1.$ So, the domain of $f$ is \n\n$$\n{D}_{f}=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,-1\\le xy\\le 1\\}.\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the partial derivatives of $f(x,y)$ with respect to $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{y}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(xy)) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-(xy)^{2}}}\\cdot x \\\\  & =\\frac{x}{\\sqrt{1-{x}^{2}{y}^{2}}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe function ${f}_{y}(x,y)$ is defined only for \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}1-{x}^{2}{y}^{2} & >0 \\\\ {x}^{2}{y}^{2} & <1 \\\\ -1<xy & <1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n So, the domain of ${f}_{y}$ is\n\n$$\n{D}_{{f}_{y}}=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,-1<xy<1\\}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we conclude that the partial derivative of $f$ with respect to $y$ exists on the set\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{D}_{f}\\cap {D}_{{f}_{y}} & =\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,-1\\le xy\\le 1\\}\\cap \\{(x,y)\\,:\\,-1<xy<1\\} \\\\  & =\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,-1<xy<1\\}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186382201 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186559237 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186382201 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186559288 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186382201 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186559319 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186382201 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186559351 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186382201 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186559580 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186382201 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186382201 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186382201 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186559580 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186382201 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "128389e5-4c10-54a7-ab3a-691a2d7a80e7" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186382208 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186445248 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186382208 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186445249 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186382208 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186445250 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186382208 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186445252 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186382208 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186445254 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186382208 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186382208 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186382208 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186445252 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186382208 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "bb805034-5a71-5881-8d6e-dc2faac4f6f6" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186408166 #edb/ref 10 :answer-field.type/checkbox #edb/ref 17592186045416] [17592186409538 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186409560 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186409538 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186409561 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186409538 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186409562 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186409538 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186