[17592186426721 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186426721 #edb/ref 1071 "Given that $f(x,y)=x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(xy),$ find the set of values $(x,y)$ on which $\\frac{∂f}{∂y}$ exists." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186426721 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "abe73768-66b7-589e-98db-9176084649e0" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186426721 #edb/ref 1074 "First, notice that $f(x,y)$ is defined when $xy>0.$ So, the domain of $f$ is \n\n$$\n{D}_{f}=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,xy>0\\}.\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the partial derivatives of $f(x,y)$ with respect to $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{y}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(xy)) \\\\  & =x\\cdot \\frac{x}{xy} \\\\  & =\\frac{x}{y}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe function ${f}_{y}(x,y)$ is defined only for\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y\\ne 0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the domain of ${f}_{y}$ is\n\n$$\n{D}_{{f}_{y}}=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,y\\ne 0\\}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we conclude that the partial derivative of $f$ with respect to $y$ exists on the set\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{D}_{f}\\cap {D}_{{f}_{y}} & =\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,xy>0\\}\\cap \\{(x,y)\\,:\\,y\\ne 0\\} \\\\  & =\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,xy>0\\}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186426722 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186426726 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186426722 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "30fe1f41-c7f8-5010-a260-ed3ef63a4abc" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186426722 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186339045 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186426723 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "574ad7f2-698f-50d6-8eee-36f6145d16e5" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186426723 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186268914 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186426724 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186426725 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186426724 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "d1a2f4bc-c2ad-5ea9-bbc9-a5f92a8d1b61" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186426724 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186125001 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186426725 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186426722 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186426725 #edb/ref 1062 #uui